.\" satl(1) - manual page for the interpreter.
.\"
.\" Kept to pure ASCII on purpose. An em dash typed as UTF-8 is three bytes that
.\" `groff -Tascii` reports as invalid input characters, even inside a comment;
.\" the escapes \(em and \(mu produce the same glyphs and render everywhere.
.\"
.\" Every flag here is one that main() actually routes; see main.cpp. A man page
.\" that documents a flag the binary does not have is worse than no man page,
.\" because a man page is believed and a --help is checked.
.TH SATL 1 2026-08-07 "satellite 0.1" "Satellite Manual"
.SH NAME
satl \- interpreter for the satellite programming language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B satl
.RB [ \-\-run ]
.IR file " [" args ...]
.br
.B satl
.RB [ \-\-repl ]
.br
.B satl \-\-where
.br
.B satl
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B satl
runs a satellite program, or opens a read\-eval\-print loop when it is given no
file. Program files conventionally end in
.IR .satl .
.PP
Satellite is a language whose generating rule is that every name the language
itself defines begins with the word
.B satellite
and a dot:
.BR satellite.capsule ,
.BR satellite.variable.string ,
.BR satellite.container.list ,
.BR satellite.console.display .
A program's own names are bare, so the two never collide and no keyword is ever
reserved out from under the user. Numbers are a single arbitrary\-precision
decimal type, which is why
.B 0.1 + 0.2
is
.B 0.3
here and not almost\-0.3.
.PP
This binary is the whole language \(em lexer, parser, resolver and
tree\-walking evaluator \(em and it links no graphical toolkit. The window is a
separate program,
.BR satl\-term (1),
which spawns this one into a pseudo\-terminal. The split is measured rather than
tidy\-minded: linking GTK4 and VTE into the interpreter made the dynamic linker
load 119 shared objects before
.B main()
on every invocation, which cost 23.4 ms of the 25.9 ms that
.B satl \-\-run hello.satl
took \(em against an interpreter whose own share of hello world is 0.3 ms, and a
bare
.B int main(){return 0;}
floor of 2.2 ms. Unlinked, the same run takes 2.5 ms.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-run " " \fIfile\fR " [" \fIargs\fR ...]
Run
.I file
and write everything it displays to standard output. The remaining
.I args
are bound to the parameter of
.B satellite.main
as a
.BR satellite.container.list < satellite.variable.string >,
with
.I file
itself as element 0 \(em a program sees its own name first, as it would in C.
.IP
Arguments are taken literally. A backslash is an ordinary character, not an
escape, so
.B C:\ehome
names the path that was typed; expanding it a second time after the shell has
already had its turn is a corruption, not a convenience.
.TP
.IR file " [" args ...]
The same as
.BR \-\-run .
Any first argument that does not begin with
.B \-
is taken as a file name.
.TP
.B \-\-repl
Read lines from standard input and evaluate them, which is also what happens
with no arguments at all. The spelled\-out form exists because
.BR satl\-term (1)
passes it explicitly when it spawns this binary into the terminal's
pseudo\-terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-where
Print the library directory this binary resolved, and exit. It answers the only
question worth asking when a program cannot find its library: which of the three
tiers under
.B ENVIRONMENT
won, and what did it point at.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary on standard error and exit 2.
.SS At the prompt
The REPL prompt is your user name and the working directory. Beyond expressions,
it takes four things:
.TP
.BR run " " \fIfile\fR " [" \fIargs\fR ...]
Run a file, through the same entry point as
.BR "satl \-\-run" .
Also spelled
.B interpret
and
.BR \-\-run ,
so whichever form is already in the user's fingers works. It exists because the
graphical terminal has no shell behind it: without it, a file could only be run
by closing the window and going back to a terminal. A nonzero status is printed
out loud, since a REPL has no exit status to carry a failure out through.
.TP
.BI :set " path value"
Set a
.B satellite.library
variable, as in
.BR ":set system.min_free_mb 2048" .
.TP
.BI :get " path"
Print one variable.
.B :vars
prints all of them.
.TP
.BR exit ", " quit ", " Ctrl\-D
Leave.
.PP
These colon commands are debugging scaffolding for poking
.BR satellite.library ,
not language syntax, and they are not guaranteed to survive.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SATELLITE_PATH
Directory to load the satellite\-language library from, overriding the two
installed locations.
.RS
.PP
The interpreter looks in three places and takes the first that exists:
.IP 1. 4
.BR $SATELLITE_PATH ,
if it is set.
.IP 2. 4
.IR .../bin/../share/satellite/lib ,
resolved from
.IR /proc/self/exe .
This is what makes an unpacked tarball work wherever it was unpacked, without
being told where that was.
.IP 3. 4
The directory compiled in as
.BR SATELLITE_LIB_DIR ,
baked from
.I prefix
when the binary was built.
.PP
.B SATELLITE_PATH
is a development override, and a correct installation does not need it \(em that
is the whole point of the ordering. Tiers 2 and 3 cover every installed case
between them; tier 1 exists so a developer can aim a built\-but\-not\-installed
binary at a working tree. If satellite cannot find its library until
.B SATELLITE_PATH
is set, then tier 2 or tier 3 is wrong, and setting the variable hides that bug
rather than fixing it. An install that requires an environment variable is a
broken install, so neither the packages nor
.B install.sh
will ever ask you to set one.
.RE
.SH FILES
Paths are shown for the default prefix,
.IR /usr/local ;
a distribution package is built with
.I /usr
instead, and
.B install.sh \-\-prefix
puts the same tree anywhere.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/satl
This program.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/satl\-term
The graphical terminal.
.BR satl\-term (1)
finds this binary as its own sibling, so the two belong in the same directory.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/satellite/lib/
The satellite\-language library \(em tier 2 above.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/satellite/examples/
Example programs.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/doc/satellite/
DESIGN.md and the copyright file, which is the licence the whole tree is under.
.SH EXAMPLES
Hello world, in a file called
.IR hello.satl :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
satellite.include(satellite)

satellite.capsule satellite.main()
{
    satellite.console.display("hello, world!")

    satellite.return(satellite)
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ satl \-\-run hello.satl
hello, world!
.EE
.RE
.PP
The bare form is the same thing, and arguments after the file belong to the
program:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ satl hello.satl \-\-verbose 'C:\ehome'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Fail a shell pipeline deliberately, with
.B satellite.return(3)
in the program:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ satl \-\-run check.satl; echo $?
3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find out which library directory is in force, which is the first thing to check
when an installation misbehaves:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ satl \-\-where
/usr/local/share/satellite/lib
.EE
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The program ran to completion, or ended in
.BR satellite.return(satellite) .
The runtime singleton means success, so it maps to 0.
.TP
.B 1
A syntax, resolution or runtime error. The report is written to standard output
along with whatever the program had already displayed, because that is where
both a reader and a test are already looking.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, or the file could not be read. It is also what the memory
watchdog exits with when available memory falls below
.BR system.min_free_mb .
.TP
.I n
.BI satellite.return( n )
with a number exits with
.IR n ,
truncated toward zero and clamped to 255. Clamped and not masked: a process exit
status carries eight bits, and masking would turn a failure of 256 into a
success.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR satl\-term (1)
.PP
.IR /usr/local/share/doc/satellite/DESIGN.md ,
which is the document the implementation is written against rather than a
description written after it.
.SH AUTHOR
The satellite project <terransatellite@gmail.com>.
